  "configuration_id": "config_025",
  "reason": "Evidence: The two completed experiments (config_004 and config_033) differ only in image encoder (resnet50 vs imagenet_vit_l), with ViT giving ~1.2% higher accuracy (0.725 vs 0.713) but slightly lower F1. Both use scvi_n200 as the RNA encoder, so we have zero information about whether a different RNA encoder (scgpt) could improve performance with the best image encoder. Question: Does scgpt, a generative RNA encoder with a different inductive bias than the variational scvi_n200, complement the ViT image features better or worse under the same transformer+weighted fusion setup? Why this is the best next step: With only 2 data points and both sharing the same RNA encoder, the RNA encoder dimension is completely unexplored. Testing scgpt with the best-performing image encoder (imagenet_vit_l) and the same aggregation/fusion (transformer/weighted) isolates the RNA encoder effect. If scgpt improves accuracy, it opens a high-value direction for the remaining 7 experiments; if not, we confirm scvi_n200 is the right choice and can focus exploitation on fusion/aggregation variations. This is the highest-information experiment to reduce uncertainty on a core design axis."
}

